# Summary
Introduced: object-key validation no longer blocks control characters, but log emission still formats keys directly, enabling log injection/forgery.
The commit removes the control-character rejection in object keys, only forbidding NUL. Keys are then used directly in trace logging strings (e.g., streaming PUT events) and written verbatim to log sinks. With this change, an attacker can store objects whose keys contain CR/LF or escape characters, causing log forging or terminal escape sequences in observability logs. This is a security-relevant integrity issue for audit logs and monitoring output, especially in environments that enable tracing.

